  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels look. The rule's status is still \"pending\", so it hasn't run yet.\n\n**Data:** the logs data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. The fields I used were `response` (a string, so 5xx is matched as text), `extension`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `host`, `request` and `url`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-b27166f6 Web logs overview\" has four panels (http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-b27166f6-web-logs-overview-fc5768b292fb):\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Bytes by extension\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-b27166f6 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` where `response.keyword` matches `5*`. It fires when that count is above 10 within a 5-minute window.\n\nThe rule has no action attached, so it will show up as an alert in Kibana but won't send a notification anywhere. Also, the sample data's timestamps may not be recent, in which case the rule won't see many documents in its 5-minute window.",
